### Enabling the HSE Oscillator on the STM32G474

The High-Speed External (HSE) clock source supports an external 4 to 48 MHz crystal/ceramic resonator or an external user clock signal [STM32G474_RM0440 p.277].

#### 1. Hardware / Bypass Selection
- **Crystal / Ceramic Resonator**: OSC_IN and OSC_OUT pins connect to the crystal with load capacitors. The bypass bit must remain cleared (`HSEBYP = 0`) [STM32G474_RM0440 p.276, p.287].
- **External Clock Source (Bypass Mode)**: An external clock signal is supplied to OSC_IN while OSC_OUT is left floating/unconnected. The bypass bit must be set (`HSEBYP = 1` in `RCC_CR`, bit 18) before enabling the oscillator [STM32G474_RM0440 p.276, p.287]. Note that `HSEBYP` can only be written when the HSE oscillator is disabled (`HSEON = 0`) [STM32G474_RM0440 p.287].

#### 2. Enabling HSE in Software
1. Set the **`HSEON`** bit (bit 16) in the Clock Control Register (`RCC_CR`, address `0x40021000`) [STM32G474_RM0440 p.287, p.288].
2. Poll the **`HSERDY`** flag (bit 17 in `RCC_CR`) until it becomes `1` before selecting HSE as the system clock source (`SYSCLK`) or as the PLL input [STM32G474_RM0440 p.277, p.287].

Example CMSIS C implementation:

```c
#include "stm32g4xx.h"

void enable_hse(void) {
    /* If using an external bypass clock, configure HSEBYP while HSE is off: */
    /* RCC->CR |= RCC_CR_HSEBYP; */

    /* Enable HSE oscillator */
    RCC->CR |= RCC_CR_HSEON;

    /* Wait until HSE is stable and ready to use */
    while ((RCC->CR & RCC_CR_HSERDY) == 0) {
        /* Wait loop (a timeout counter can be added in production code) */
    }
}
```

---

### Meaning of the `HSERDY` Flag

`HSERDY` is bit 17 of the `RCC_CR` register [STM32G474_RM0440 p.287]:

- **Hardware Status Flag**: `HSERDY` is a read-only bit set and cleared by hardware [STM32G474_RM0440 p.287].
- **Oscillator Stability Indicator**:
  - `0`: HSE oscillator is not ready / not stable [STM32G474_RM0440 p.287].
  - `1`: HSE oscillator is stable and ready to supply clock signals [STM32G474_RM0440 p.287].
- **Clock Release**: After setting `HSEON`, the clock is gated internally and not released until `HSERDY` is asserted by hardware [STM32G474_RM0440 p.277].
- **Disabling Behavior**: When `HSEON` is cleared by software to stop the HSE oscillator, `HSERDY` goes low after 6 HSE clock cycles [STM32G474_RM0440 p.287].
- **Interrupt Capability**: If software enables the `HSERDYIE` bit in the Clock Interrupt Enable Register (`RCC_CIER`), hardware generates an interrupt when `HSERDY` becomes set; the pending flag `HSERDYF` in `RCC_CIFR` is cleared by writing `HSERDYC` in `RCC_CICR` [STM32G474_RM0440 p.277, p.296].

